      "id": "word-se--irse-marker",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "se",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; it forms irse, 'to leave' or 'to go away'",
      "noteEn": "Ir means 'to go.' With a matching short word such as me, te, se, or nos, irse focuses on departure: se va means that someone or something leaves or goes away.",
      "example": { "es": "El último autobús se va a las once.", "en": "The last bus leaves at eleven." },
      "tags": ["word", "verbs", "irse", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-va--leaves",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "va",
      "answerEn": "it leaves; it goes away (with se)",
      "noteEn": "Va is the present form of ir used with one other person or thing. In se va, the added se makes irse and shifts the focus from movement toward somewhere to departure from the current place or state.",
      "example": { "es": "El dolor se va poco a poco.", "en": "The pain goes away little by little." },
      "tags": ["word", "verbs", "irse", "A2"]
    },
